# promise: crash-extensions-renderer-reused-on-exit
# A renderer host that starts its next process inside the notice that its last one ended, before Chrome's
# extensions system has heard of the end. That system kept the old process's entry when the new process was
# created (and sent it nothing), dropped the entry when its turn in the notice came, and found the new process
# untracked once it launched: CHECK in extensions::RendererStartupHelper::OnRenderProcessLaunched (0.1.52 and
# 0.1.54 crash reports). Chromium handled it on Android and ChromeOS only; renderer-startup-reused-process.patch
# gives that process the extensions' state on the Mac too. The hook (cra://debug?rendererReuse=1) ends a fresh
# renderer twice, the second time starting the next one from inside the notice, ahead of the extensions system.
budget rss=900 stalls50=30
extension ${FIXTURES}/Extension
seed settings.json {"hasSeenImportPrompt": true, "toolbarDefaultMigrated": true}
cra://open?url=${PAGE}?n=before
expect-log loaded 127\.0\.0\.1 \(HTTP 200\)
wait 1
cra://debug?rendererReuse=1
expect-log renderer reuse: starts the next process inside the exit notice
expect-log extensions: a renderer launched untracked; initializing it
expect-log renderer reuse: the process started in the exit notice is running
cra://open?url=${PAGE}?n=after
expect-log GET /page\.html\?n=after
reject-log fault:
